Time for the once a decade Jeremiad?
1.=A0 The perfect is the mortal enemy of the good.=A0 Are you sure Lojban n=
eeds more fixing (or even all it seems to have gotten)?=A0 Do you have a cl=
ear idea what Lojban is supposed to do and have you really tried to do thes=
e things with it?=A0 (A large part of what I see every day, aside from new =
words, are reinventing the wheel without looking at the wheels already turn=
ing. I know what lacks I see and know how to fix them fairly easily -- and =
backward compatibly -- but the next problem gets in the way).=20

2.=A0 Who has the authority to make a reasonably final decision on what is =
a real problem and what is a solution?=A0 A person?=A0 a group?=A0 the comm=
unity (how polled?)?=A0 Will everybody (anybody) heed?=A0 Most proposed pro=
blems gets solved (several times) fairly quickly, then forgotten until (and=
 even after) they come up again.=A0 So, where is the record of problems rai=
sed and solutions accepted (note: the searchable archives have proven fairl=
y ineffective for this)?=A0 And who maintains it, if anyone?

3.=A0 [Expanding on 1]=A0 While learning the mechanics of Lojban, no real e=
ffort has been made to teach Lojbanism, the philosophy (as it were, and in =
a good sense) behind the language.=A0 No one is taken in for long by the ol=
der saw about testing the Whorf Hypothesis and the bit about thinking logic=
ally doesn't last much longer (if as long, come to think of it).=A0 So peop=
le bring all sorts of ideas or ideals to Lojban and, when it does immediate=
ly deal with them, either quit in disgust or try to fix it, never noting th=
at what they are out to do is nothing something Lojban was out to do.=A0 So=
 many problems are presented and taken seriously that are just beside the p=
oint, but are indistinguishable in their fervor from central ones.

4.=A0 While anybody may have a good idea and no one should be censored, som=
e people have track records that entitle their views to a more careful cons=
ideration that Joe Newby's -- even though Joe may eventually turn out to be=
 right.=A0 This is a difficult balancing act but an essential one if progre=
ss is not to be constantly diverted by pseudoproblems=A0 or wild-eyed (in r=
etrospect) theories.=A0=20

5.=A0 Enough of Lojban is settled syntactically and semantically to do as a=
 functioning language for 99% of the purposes one might have in mind (much =
more so than, say, English).=A0 More time might be spent on getting some de=
cent teaching aids going at all level (not to disparage what there are, but=
 more and more diverse ones are needed).=A0 Prolix Lojban is difficult main=
ly because of its vocabulary, which has traditionally been ram-fed, rather =
than in the modern traditional way.=A0 But as Pimsleur and the like show, y=
ou can get a long way with a hundred words and the basic grammar (both of w=
hich are much simpler in Lojban than in French or -- God help us -- English=
).=A0 If you come to a problem, you can look it up or play around a bit -- =
getting the message across even if the Lojban is a bit iffy.=A0 (Toki pona,=
 with 120 words, give or take, and a half-page grammar, has managed to say =
just about everything anyone has tried to day so far and has managed to be
 understood at least as well as my French.)

6.=A0 Lojban probably isn/t your life, but work at it proportionally to you=
r sense of commitment.

--=20
